# daily-curator alignment plan

All 11 issues target `skills/daily-curator/`. The other skills
(`article-to-insights`, `profile-audit`) are out of scope. This is a
docs-and-contract alignment of an existing Python 3 stdlib skill, not a
new app. Product name is Braid; the skill name is `daily-curator`.

The skill already has the right mechanics. The work is to delete the
leftover v2 cutover, make the docs match what the scripts actually do,
add one first-run bootstrap and a dry-run that can verify content
without mutating state, then rewrite `SKILL.md` around the daily path
and lock it with a fixture harness.

---

## Current vs target

```
skills/daily-curator/
  SKILL.md                         daily path only after #9/#10
  agents/openai.yaml
  references/
    curated-feeds.md               bootstrap seed (already)
    taste-template.md              0.4 floor, no Suggested-track (#8)
    scoring-and-filtering.md       present-tense + feed-health rationale
    output-format.md               weekly format only (daily lives in SKILL.md)
    feed-management.md             NEW (#10)
    weekly.md                      NEW (#10)
  scripts/
    canon.py                       shared primitives (URL, dates, seen.txt)
    curate.py                      prepare / select / mark-seen / roundup
                                   + bootstrap subcommand (#4)
    health.py                      feed-health.json
    verify-run.py                  + --dry-run (#6)
    import-opml.sh
    migrate.py                     DELETE (#2)
    tests/test_curator.py          drop TestMigrate; keep the rest
  evals/                           NEW (#11/#12)
    harness.py
    fixtures/...
    test_scenarios.py
```

State root is `$DAILY_CURATOR_HOME` (default `~/.daily-curator`). Script
root is the skill directory. Those two must stay distinct in every
documented command.

---

## Shared types and owned files

`canon.py` stays the single source of truth for URL canonicalization,
date parsing, `state_home()`, JSONL read tolerance, and the `seen.txt`
ledger. Do not fork those helpers into bootstrap, verify, or the eval
harness.

**User-managed**

| File | Role |
|---|---|
| `feeds.txt` | one RSS/Atom URL per line; seeded on first run |
| `taste.md` | LLM-read taste profile; copied from the template |
| `negative-anchors.txt` | optional; extends built-in title regexes |

**Skill-owned (create empty on demand)**

| File | Role |
|---|---|
| `seen.txt` | JSONL `{url, date_shown}` — dedup only, 30-day prune |
| `shown.jsonl` | JSONL shown items **including score** — weekly source |
| `feed-health.json` | per-feed `{first_seen, last_ok, last_status, last_alert}` |
| `digests/YYYY-MM-DD.md` | real daily digest; also the same-day idempotency key |
| `digests/YYYY-MM-DD-weekly.md` | weekly heartbeat (distinct name) |
| `tmp/candidates-YYYY-MM-DD.json` | `prepare` output; date = UTC today |
| `tmp/scored.json` | agent writes this after scoring |
| `tmp/selected.json` | `select` stdout redirected here |
| `tmp/seen-snapshot.json` | pre-run seen set for the verifier |
| `tmp/digest-YYYY-MM-DD.md` | dry-run digest only |

`shown.jsonl` row shape (already implemented by `append_shown`):

```
{url, title, source_bucket, published, summary, score, date_shown}
```

---

## Two roots, one daily pipeline

```
$SKILL_DIR                          $DAILY_CURATOR_HOME
  scripts/curate.py                   feeds.txt  taste.md
  scripts/verify-run.py               seen.txt   shown.jsonl
  references/                         feed-health.json
                                      digests/   tmp/
```

Commands always run from `$SKILL_DIR`. State paths always go through
`--home` / `$DAILY_CURATOR_HOME`. Date in filenames is UTC today
(`canon.today_utc()`), same clock `prepare` already uses.

Daily sequence (agent judgment in brackets):

```
bootstrap → idempotency guard → prepare
  → [score 0–1 into tmp/scored.json]
  → select  (stdout > tmp/selected.json)
  → [render digest]
  → mark-seen → verify → deliver
```

Documented `select` today claims it writes `tmp/selected.json`. It does
not — it prints JSON. Every later issue that quotes a command must
include the redirect.

